  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Thunder.Dbf now targets .NET 11 as well as .NET 10, and allocates less on seeks, appends and reads. Two fixes are worth upgrading for: on the .NET 11 runtime a double was converted to decimal exactly (1.23 became 1.2299999999999999822...), which could round currency wrongly and miss seeks; and a FoxPro `.cdx` left torn by a killed `DeferIndexWrites` batch could only be repaired in Visual FoxPro, where `RebuildIndex()` now repairs it.\n\n**Upgrading from 0.4.4:** nothing in the public API changed, and apps on .NET 10 keep the .NET 10 build. Three things behave differently:\n- A double meets a decimal (currency, `.ntx` keys, expressions, `ModifyStructure`, `GetDecimal`) as the number it prints as, on both runtimes: on .NET 11 that's what .NET 10 gave for most values, and a double with 16 or 17 significant digits, or a float with 8 or 9, now keeps them, where .NET 10 rounded them to 15 and 7.\n- A `.cdx` whose tag trees are torn, but whose tag headers and roots read, is refused as rebuildable instead of unreadable: `RebuildIndex()` on an exclusive open, or `MaintainIndex(path, rebuild: true)`, rebuilds it. Writes are still refused until then.\n- The `IndexWouldGoStale` message for an unfinished `DeferIndexWrites` batch says the batch didn't finish (it failed partway or its program stopped) instead of saying it was cut off. Code that matches on the message text needs the new wording; the error code is unchanged.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **A double is taken as the number it prints as, on .NET 11 too.** .NET 11 converts a double to decimal exactly, so the double 1.23 became 1.2299999999999999822..., where .NET 10 gave 1.23. On .NET 11 that rounded a currency value given as the double 0.00015 to 0.0001 instead of 0.0002, and could miss seeks and change `ModifyStructure`'s loss checks. Thunder.Dbf now goes through the double's shortest text (as it already did for numeric fields) wherever a double meets a decimal: currency writes and seeks, `.ntx` keys, expressions, `ModifyStructure`, `DbfDataReader.GetDecimal` on a double field, and SQL `REAL` values coming back through Thunder.Dbf.Sql. This affects apps running 0.4.4 or earlier on the .NET 11 runtime. On .NET 10 it changes two things, so that both runtimes now store the same value: a double with 16 or 17 significant digits keeps them, where .NET 10's conversion rounded it to 15 (the double 1234567890123456 was stored as 1234567890123460), and a float with 8 or 9 significant digits keeps them, where it was rounded to 7 (the float 12345678 was stored as 12345680).\n\n- **`RebuildIndex()` repairs a `.cdx` whose tag trees a cut-off batch left torn.** A `DeferIndexWrites` batch killed while it rewrote pages that already existed could leave a tree that reaches a page twice. Its note stopped writes, as designed, but nothing in Thunder.Dbf could repair the index: the exclusive open kept no index, `RebuildIndex()` said there was none, and `MaintainIndex(path, rebuild: true)` called it unreadable. Only Visual FoxPro's REINDEX could. Now, when every tag's header still reads and every tag's root is its own page, as a cut-off batch leaves them, the index is refused as rebuildable: nothing but a rebuild of every tag may write to it, a journal note's replay included, and the rebuild builds every tag from the table in new pages. It frees none of the torn trees' pages, since which ones are still in use can't be told, and starts the free list over; the old pages stay in the file, unused. This affects 0.4.4 too.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Thunder.Dbf, Thunder.Dbf.Sql and the `thunder-dbf` tool now target .NET 11 as well as .NET 10.** Apps on .NET 10 (the long-term support release) keep working as before; apps on .NET 11 get a build compiled for it. The web service, samples and benchmarks now run on .NET 11. Building the repository needs the .NET 11 SDK, and the tests run on both runtimes.\n- **Seeks, appends and reads allocate less.** An `.mdx` seek reads each index page into a pooled buffer and decodes numeric keys without building strings: 10,000 seeks allocate 20 MB, down from 63 MB. Every write claims its locks and walks its indexes without LINQ or closures (`.mdx` appends allocate 24% less), and `.cdx` pages are cached as written instead of copied (19% less). Numeric, date and memo-pointer fields are parsed straight from their bytes; a plain number is no longer checked digit by digit through strings, because a decimal holds any number of 28 digits or fewer exactly. Every field reads exactly as before, errors included.\n- **A batch of deferred index writes that failed no longer reads as a crash.** When a `DeferIndexWrites` batch stopped partway (a damaged index, a full disk), the next writer was told the batch \"was cut off\", which pointed at a killed program. It now says the batch didn't finish because it failed partway or its program stopped. The refusal itself is unchanged.\n- **The benchmarks cover writes, seeks and garbage collection.** New benchmarks time appends that keep a dBASE IV `.mdx` and a FoxPro `.cdx` up to date and seeks through an `.mdx`, and `-- gc` reports GC pause time, collections and allocations for a mixed workload. Run them with `-f net10.0` or `-f net11.0` to compare runtimes; on .NET 11 they run in-process, because BenchmarkDotNet 0.15 can't yet start a separate .NET 11 process.\n\nFull changelog: https://github.com/pthunder/Thunder.Dbf/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md",
